WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.947
Now we are ready to run the complete clinic schema.

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:08.772
This is an instructor-guided reference, not a script you need to memorize.

00:00:08.772 --> 00:00:13.492
Before clicking Run, scan the script and count the CREATE TABLE statements.

00:00:13.492 --> 00:00:17.807
Then look for primary keys, unique business identifiers, foreign keys, and check constraints.

00:00:17.807 --> 00:00:25.112
Run the schema in the browser and confirm that PostgreSQL creates the tables successfully.

00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:32.092
The order matters because parent tables need to exist before child tables can reference them.

00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:35.107
If you copy the script into pgAdmin, use it as a reading exercise.

00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:38.662
Trace each foreign key back to its parent table.

00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:45.117
The main goal is to see how the conceptual and logical design decisions become executable PostgreSQL.

00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:50.517
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:55.587
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:55.587 --> 00:00:58.457
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:05.087
In a real setting such as an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines,

00:01:05.087 --> 00:01:11.862
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:11.862 --> 00:01:14.402
before discussing syntax.

00:01:14.402 --> 00:01:20.098
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
